{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"The paper addresses AMR-to-text generation by leveraging synchronous node replacement grammar.","source_span":"This paper addresses the task of AMR-to-text generation by leveraging synchronous node replacement grammar."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"Evaluated on SemEval-2016 Task 8, the method gives a BLEU score of 25.62, the best reported so far.","source_span":"Evaluated on SemEval-2016 Task 8, our method gives a BLEU score of 25.62, which is the best reported so far."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"During training, graph-to-string rules are learned using a heuristic extraction algorithm.","source_span":"During training, graph-to-string rules are learned using a heuristic extraction algorithm."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"At test time, a graph transducer is applied to collapse input AMRs and generate output sentences.","source_span":"At test time, a graph transducer is applied to collapse input AMRs and generate output sentences."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"jeff2016amrgen transform a given AMR graph into a spanning tree before translating it to a sentence using a tree-to-string transducer.","source_span":"jeff2016amrgen transform a given AMR graph into a spanning tree, before translating it to a sentence using a tree-to-string transducer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"song-EtAl:2016:EMNLP2016 cast the task of finding a sequence of disjoint rules to transduce an AMR graph into a sentence as a traveling salesman problem, using local features and a language model to rank candidate sentences.","source_span":"They cast the task of finding a sequence of disjoint rules to transduce an AMR graph into a sentence as a traveling salesman problem, using local features and a language model to rank candidate sentences."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"The system gives a BLEU score of 25.62 on LDC2015E86, the state-of-the-art on this dataset.","source_span":"It gives a BLEU score of 25.62 on LDC2015E86, which is the state-of-the-art on this dataset."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"The system makes use of a log-linear model with real-valued features, tuned using MERT, and beam search decoding.","source_span":"Our system makes use of a log-linear model with real-valued features, tuned using MERT BIBREF19 , and beam search decoding."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"There are three types of rules in the system: induced rules, concept rules, and graph glue rules.","source_span":"There are three types of rules in our system, namely induced rules, concept rules and graph glue rules."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"A concept rule is used in case no induced rule can cover the node, where F is a single node in the input AMR graph and E is a morphological string of the node concept.","source_span":"For a concept rule, $F$ is a single node in the input AMR graph, and $E$ is a morphological string of the node concept. A concept rule is used in case no induced rule can cover the node."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"An example concept rule created from the verbalization list is \"(k / keep-01 :ARG1 (p / peace)) ||| peacekeeping\".","source_span":"one concept rule created from the verbalization list is “(k / keep-01 :ARG1 (p / peace)) $|||$ peacekeeping”"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","statement":"Graph glue rules concatenate non-terminal nodes connected with an edge when no induced rules can be applied, with three glue rules defined for each type of edge label.","source_span":"we define graph glue rules to concatenate non-terminal nodes connected with an edge, when no induced rules can be applied. Three glue rules are defined for each type of edge label."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What BLEU score did the proposed AMR-to-text generation system achieve on SemEval-2016 Task 8, and how did it rank?","answer":"It achieved a BLEU score of 25.62, which was the best reported score so far on that benchmark."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","fact_refs":[6],"question":"On which dataset did the system achieve a state-of-the-art BLEU score of 25.62?","answer":"LDC2015E86."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What tuning method, decoding technique, and model type does the AMR-to-text system use?","answer":"It uses a log-linear model with real-valued features, tuned with MERT, combined with beam search decoding."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How does jeff2016amrgen's approach convert an AMR graph into a sentence?","answer":"It first transforms the AMR graph into a spanning tree, then translates that tree into a sentence using a tree-to-string transducer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What are the three types of rules used in the synchronous node replacement grammar system for AMR-to-text generation?","answer":"Induced rules, concept rules, and graph glue rules."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How does song-EtAl:2016:EMNLP2016 frame the problem of transducing an AMR graph into a sentence?","answer":"They frame it as a traveling salesman problem: finding a sequence of disjoint graph-fragment-to-string rules, ranked using local features and a language model."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0127","fact_refs":[9,10],"question":"When is a concept rule used instead of an induced rule, and what does the example \"(k / keep-01 :ARG1 (p / peace)) ||| peacekeeping\" illustrate?","answer":"A concept rule is used when no induced rule can cover a node, mapping a single AMR node (F) to a morphological string (E); the example shows the AMR node for \"keep-01\" with ARG1 \"peace\" mapping to the word \"peacekeeping\"."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","statement":"Opinion recommendation is the task of jointly predicting a custom review with a rating score that a user would give to a product or service, using existing reviews/ratings for that product by other users and the reviews the user has given to other products.","source_span":"We present opinion recommendation, a novel task of jointly predicting a custom review with a rating score that a certain user would give to a certain product or service, given existing reviews and rating scores to the product or service by other users, and the reviews that the user has given to other products and services."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","statement":"The model uses a single neural network to model users and products, generating customised product representations using a deep memory network, from which customised ratings and reviews are jointly constructed.","source_span":"We use a single neural network to model users and products, capturing their correlation and generating customised product representations using a deep memory network, from which customised ratings and reviews are constructed jointly."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","statement":"Results on Yelp.com data show the opinion recommendation system gives ratings closer to real user ratings than Yelp's own ratings.","source_span":"Results show that our opinion recommendation system gives ratings that are closer to real user ratings on Yelp.com data compared with Yelp's own ratings"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","statement":"There are three types of inputs for the task: reviews of the target product, reviews of the user on other products, and other users' reviews on other products; and two types of outputs: a customized rating score and a customized review.","source_span":"There are three types of inputs for our task, namely the reviews of the target product, the reviews of the user on other products, and other users reviews on other products, and two types of outputs, namely a customized rating score and a customized review."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","statement":"Neighbor users are identified by collaborative filtering to address potential sparsity of a user's history reviews, and a vector representation is learned using a neural neighborhood model.","source_span":"to address potential sparsity of a user's history reviews, neighbor users are identified by collaborative filtering BIBREF8 , and a vector representation is learned by using a neural neighborhood model, which consolidates their history reviews."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","statement":"A recurrent neural network is used to model the semantic content of each review.","source_span":"In particular, we use a recurrent neural network to model the semantic content of each review."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","statement":"PangL05 pioneered the review rating prediction task by treating it as a classification/regression problem.","source_span":"PangL05 pioneered this task by regarding it as a classification/regression problem."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","statement":"TangQLY15 proposed a neural network model to predict the rating score using both lexical semantic and user model.","source_span":"Recently, TangQLY15 proposed a neural network model to predict the rating score by using both lexical semantic and user model."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","statement":"Examples of popular review websites include IMDB for movies, Epinions for products, and Yelp for services.","source_span":"Popular examples include IMDB on the movie domain, Epinions on the product domain, and Yelp on the service domain."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is the task of opinion recommendation as defined in this paper?","answer":"It is jointly predicting a custom review and rating score that a given user would give a given product or service, based on other users' existing reviews/ratings for that product and the reviews the user has written for other products and services."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are the three types of inputs and two types of outputs used in the opinion recommendation task?","answer":"The three inputs are reviews of the target product, the user's reviews of other products, and other users' reviews of other products; the two outputs are a customized rating score and a customized review."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How does the opinion recommendation model address sparsity in a user's review history?","answer":"It identifies neighbor users via collaborative filtering and learns a vector representation from their consolidated history reviews using a neural neighborhood model."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How did the opinion recommendation system's predicted ratings compare to Yelp's own ratings on Yelp.com data?","answer":"The system's ratings were closer to real user ratings than Yelp's own assigned ratings."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Who pioneered the review rating prediction task, and how did they approach it?","answer":"PangL05 pioneered it by treating review rating prediction as a classification/regression problem."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0128","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What are three example review websites mentioned, and which domain does each cover?","answer":"IMDB covers movies, Epinions covers products, and Yelp covers services."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","statement":"The gazetteers constructed for TWNERTC using a graph crawler algorithm on Freebase contain approximately 300K entities with thousands of fine-grained entity types under 77 different domains.","source_span":"The constructed gazetteers contains approximately 300K entities with thousands of fine-grained entity types under 77 different domains."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","statement":"Fine-grained entity types in TWNERTC are mapped to four coarse-grained types: person, loc, org, misc.","source_span":"we map fine-grained entity types to the equivalent four coarse-grained types: person, loc, org, misc."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","statement":"Six different versions of the TWNERTC dataset were constructed.","source_span":"we construct six different dataset versions and evaluate the quality of annotations by comparing ground truths from human annotators."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","statement":"Reuters Corpus Volume 1 (RCV1) consists of manually categorized 800K news stories with over 100 sub-categories under three different main categories.","source_span":"The largest and the most popular dataset among them is Reuters Corpus Volume 1 (RCV1) which consists of manually categorized 800K news stories with over 100 sub-categories under three different main categories BIBREF16 ."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","statement":"The most popular Turkish NER dataset, introduced by Gökhan et al., contains articles from newspapers totaling approximately 500K words and is manually annotated with coarse-grained entity types.","source_span":"The most popular Turkish NER dataset is introduced by Gökhan et al. BIBREF18 . This dataset contains articles from newspapers, approximately 500K words, and is manually annotated with coarse-grained entity types."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","statement":"Tatar and Çiçekli's coarse-grained Turkish NER dataset contains only 55K words.","source_span":"Tatar and Çiçekli propose another coarse-grained NER dataset BIBREF19 ; however, it contains only 55K words which makes this dataset to less preferable than previous dataset."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","statement":"The classic NER task is defined in MUC and CoNLL conferences with coarse-grained entity types: person, location, organization, and misc.","source_span":"the classical NER task is defined in MUC BIBREF6 and CoNLL BIBREF7 conferences with coarse-grained entity types: person, location, organization and misc."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","statement":"Steinmetz et al. published benchmark evaluations comparing three datasets using semantic information from knowledge bases: DBpedia Spotlight, KORE50, and the Wikilinks Corpus.","source_span":"Steinmetz et al. BIBREF11 published benchmark evaluations that compare three datasets that use semantic information from KBs: DBpedia Spotlight BIBREF3 , KORE50 BIBREF2 , BIBREF12 and the Wikilinks Corpus BIBREF13 ."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","statement":"TWNERTC uses Turkish Wikipedia dumps as the text source and Freebase to construct large-scale gazetteers mapping fine-grained types to entities.","source_span":"We use Turkish Wikipedia dumps as the text source and Freebase to construct a large-scale gazetteers to map fine-grained types to entities."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How many entities and domains do the gazetteers constructed for the TWNERTC dataset cover?","answer":"The gazetteers contain approximately 300K entities with thousands of fine-grained entity types under 77 different domains."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What four coarse-grained entity types are the fine-grained types mapped to in the TWNERTC dataset?","answer":"Person, location (loc), organization (org), and miscellaneous (misc)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How many different versions of the TWNERTC dataset were constructed?","answer":"Six different dataset versions were constructed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What does the Reuters Corpus Volume 1 (RCV1) dataset consist of?","answer":"It consists of 800K manually categorized news stories with over 100 sub-categories under three main categories."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Who introduced the most popular Turkish NER dataset, and how large is it?","answer":"Gökhan et al. introduced it; it contains newspaper articles totaling approximately 500K words, manually annotated with coarse-grained entity types."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0129","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What text source and knowledge base were used to build the TWNERTC dataset's gazetteers?","answer":"Turkish Wikipedia dumps were used as the text source, and Freebase was used to construct the large-scale gazetteers mapping fine-grained types to entities."}
